The realization of immersive interactive environments requires ubiquitous touch sensory interfaces that are cheap, simple, and self-sustainable. While the backscatter technique has enabled ultra-low cost and battery-free touch sensing, it still relies on external excitation signals as both the energy source and the sensing carrier, which sacrifices self-sustainability and reliability. We present PActor, a purely self-sustainable system that operates the “sense-and-transmit” pipeline without relying on either the batteries or external signals. Specifically, we show that it is possible for a sensor to generate its own signal and actively encode sensing information on the signal, using only zero-power components. To show PActor’s feasibility, we build a hardware prototype and demonstrate its ability in various environments, including dynamic and NLoS scenarios. The experimental results show that PActor supports reliable detection of touch events with a > 98% accuracy. It also supports touch speed and force sensing, with accuracy of 4.6 mm/s and 1.64 N, respectively.